.TH TNT-MESSAGE-LOG 5 "2026-10-15" "TNT 1.8.0"
.SH NAME
tnt-message-log \- TNT public message log format
.SH DESCRIPTION
TNT stores public room history in an append-only UTF-8 file named
.IR messages.log .
This page defines version 3 of the record format used for replay, search,
export, validation, and recovery.
.P
A version 3 file begins with the marker line
.BR "#tnt-message-log v3" .
A file that begins with
.B "#tnt-message-log v2"
is version 2, and a file with no marker line is version 1.
The server migrates both in place at start.
See
.B VERSIONS
below.
.SH FORMAT
Each record occupies one newline-terminated line.
It has exactly four fields and three literal
.B |
separators:
.P
.EX
#tnt\-message\-log v3
2026-05-27T12:34:56Z|alice|hello|v
2026-05-27T12:35:02Z|bob|hi alice|
.EE
.P
The fields are:
.TP
.I timestamp
A UTC timestamp in the form
.BR YYYY-MM-DDTHH:MM:SSZ .
.TP
.I username
A nonempty UTF-8 string of at most 63 bytes.
.TP
.I content
A nonempty UTF-8 string of at most 1023 bytes, escaped as described below.
.TP
.I flags
Zero or more lowercase ASCII letters, each a fact about the record.
The only flag defined so far is
.BR v :
the sender proved its identity when the message was sent.
That is a message from a signed-in interactive session, an exec
.B post
under the sender's own handle with its bound SSH key, or a
.B message.post
from the module holding
.BR identity:write .
For an action the sender is the actor named at the start of the content.
Readers ignore letters they do not know, so a later version may add flags
without making older records or older readers wrong.
Any other byte makes the record invalid.
.P
Neither string field may contain
.BR | ,
a C0 or C1 control character, or DEL.
Every record, including the final record, must end with a newline.
.SH CONTENT ENCODING
The stored content field is escaped so that a message may hold a line feed
while records stay one per line:
.TP
.B \e\e
A single backslash.
.TP
.B \en
A line feed.
.P
A backslash followed by anything else is invalid.
The 1023-byte limit applies to the escaped form, so a line feed costs two
bytes on disk.
A line feed is the only C0 or C1 control character a message may carry;
usernames still reject every one of them, as do
.B exec post
and the module protocol.
.P
Readers that present message text unescape these two sequences.
Exec
.B dump
and the recovery modes emit the escaped form, one record per line.
Exec
.B dump
leaves out the flags field, as it did before version 3; see
.BR tnt\-exec (7).
.SH VALIDATION
One parser is used by server replay, interactive search, exec
.BR dump ,
offline checking, and recovery.
A record is invalid if it:
.IP \(bu 2
does not have exactly three separators, with nonempty timestamp, username,
and content fields;
.IP \(bu 2
has a flags field with anything but lowercase ASCII letters;
.IP \(bu 2
does not end with a newline;
.IP \(bu 2
contains invalid UTF-8, a C0 or C1 control character other than an escaped
line feed, or DEL;
.IP \(bu 2
contains a backslash escape the encoding does not define;
.IP \(bu 2
exceeds either field limit; or
.IP \(bu 2
has an invalid or out-of-window timestamp.
.P
At scan time,
a timestamp is accepted from 3650 days before the current time
through 24 hours after it.
.P
Normal readers skip invalid records and continue with later records.
A truncated final append is therefore ignored rather than replayed.
.SH WRITING
The server writes the marker line when it creates the file, so a new
installation is version 3 from its first message.
.P
Before appending a record, the server replaces
.BR | ,
line feed, and carriage return in a username with
.BR _ .
In content it replaces the separator and a carriage return with spaces, and
keeps a line feed, which the record layer then escapes.
Interactive composition and paste can produce a line feed;
.B exec post
and module-created messages cannot, because both refuse every C0 byte.
Timestamps are written in UTC.
The flags field is
.B v
for a verified sender and empty otherwise.
.P
Interactive public messages, public room notices, exec posts, and
module-created messages use this log.
Private messages and private inbox state are never written to it.
.SH ROTATION
After an append makes the active file larger than 10 MiB, TNT closes it and
renames it to
.IR messages.log.1 .
A later append creates a new
.IR messages.log .
The next rotation replaces the existing
.IR messages.log.1 .
.P
Replay, search, and exec
.B dump
read only the active
.IR messages.log .
They do not merge the rotated file.
.SH RECOVERY
.B tnt
provides two offline maintenance modes.
Neither mode starts the SSH server.
.TP
.BI "tnt \-\-log\-check " file
Validate every physical record and write a summary to standard output.
.TP
.BI "tnt \-\-log\-recover " file
Write the marker line and each valid record in canonical version 3 form to
standard output.
Canonical flags are the ones this version knows, so an unknown flag is
dropped.
Write the summary to standard error.
The source file is not modified.
.P
The summary has these line-oriented fields:
.P
.EX
path /var/lib/tnt/messages.log
format_version 3
records_seen 120
valid_records 119
invalid_records 1
first_invalid_line 120
.EE
.P
.B format_version
reports the version of the file as read.
The marker line is not counted as a record.
.B first_invalid_line
is 0 when no invalid record was found.
Both modes return 0 when all records are valid.
They return 1 when invalid records or
I/O errors occur.
.P
Given a version 1 or 2 file, both modes upgrade each record as migration
does before validating it, so recovery output means what the original
meant.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I state-directory/messages.log
Active public message log.
.TP
.I state-directory/messages.log.1
Single rotated predecessor of the active log.
.TP
.I state-directory/messages.log.v1.bak
Copy of a version 1 log as it stood before migration.
.TP
.I state-directory/messages.log.v2.bak
Copy of a version 2 log as it stood before migration.
.SH VERSIONS
The control-free UTF-8 framing, one record per line, is stable throughout
TNT 1.x.
Version 2 (TNT 1.3.2) adds the marker line and the content encoding above.
Version 3 (TNT 1.7.0) adds the flags field, so whether a sender was verified
is recorded when the message is sent rather than guessed later from who
holds a handle now.
.P
At start, the server migrates an older log.
It copies the file to
.I messages.log.v1.bak
or
.IR messages.log.v2.bak ,
writes a replacement containing the version 3 marker line followed by every
record upgraded, and renames that over the original.
Upgrading a version 1 record escapes its backslashes; upgrading any record
appends an empty flags field, so no older message is marked verified.
A version 1 record whose escaped content would exceed the field limit is
left in the backup only.
.P
Migration is idempotent: a file that already carries the version 3 marker
line is not touched, so a restart, a restore, or a copy cannot upgrade a
record twice.
Escaping is not reversible by a second pass, which is why the marker
travels inside the file rather than beside it.
.P
Version 1 migration exists because a version 1 record could already contain
a backslash, as in
.BR C:\enew .
Read under version 2 rules without escaping, that content would decode to
.B C:
followed by a line feed and
.BR ew .
.P
TNT 1.6 and earlier read a version 3 log as having no valid records,
because every record has one separator too many for them.
TNT 1.3.2 through 1.6 also take the version 3 marker for a version 1 log
and migrate it again.
To go back to an older release, stop the server and restore
.IR messages.log.v2.bak ,
which lacks every message sent since the upgrade.
The rotated
.I messages.log.1
is not migrated and may still be version 2.
.SH CAVEATS
The file is append-only operational state, not an editable configuration
file.
Manual edits can create invalid UTF-8, missing final newlines, extra
separators, or timestamps outside the accepted window.
.P
Recovery output can be redirected to a new file.
Replacing a live log can
race a server append.
Stop the server before replacing or compacting its active log.
.P
Earlier TNT 1.3.0 builds could write records containing C0 or C1 control
characters.
The current reader skips those records.
Use
.B \-\-log\-check
before an upgrade and
.B \-\-log\-recover
to produce a control-free log.
.SH EXAMPLES
Check a stopped server's active log:
.P
.EX
tnt \-\-log\-check /var/lib/tnt/messages.log
.EE
.P
Recover valid records into a separate file:
.P
.EX
tnt \-\-log\-recover /var/lib/tnt/messages.log \
    >recovered.messages.log
.EE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR tntctl (1),
.BR tnt\-chat (7),
.BR tnt\-exec (7),
.BR tnt (8)
